@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
allowmixing
hide circle
hide empty methods
title G2 · Package structure: what a tool follows from the public declarations
top to bottom direction
folder "AIL package\nDescribes a chosen part of an API completely" as pkg #E8F3EB {
  class "surface\nWhich part of the API is described?" as surface {
    Names the API subset and release
  }
  class "exports\nWhere a tool starts reading" as roots {
    Lists the public declarations
  }
  class "Private support\nDeclarations reached by following references" as support {
    Their contracts still apply
  }
  class "environment\nWhere these contracts apply" as env {
    Exact target and platform
    optional refinements
  }
  class "Dependency selections\nWhich other definitions to read" as pins {
    Names versions and exact bytes
  }
  surface --> roots : states which API\nthese declarations describe
  roots --> support : leads the tool to\nfurther declarations
  roots --> env : names where each declaration\nis offered, where applicable
  support --> env : stays available wherever\na referring declaration needs it,\nwhere applicable
  env -[hidden]down-> pins
}
$package(imports, "Imported packages\nName, version, document digest")
$catalogue(catalogues, "Profile and ABI entries\nIdentifier, version, manifest digest")
pins --> imports : selects package documents\nwhen imports are needed
pins --> catalogues : selects manifests\ndefining the rules
legend bottom
  Schematic package structure. Non-normative: L2–L3, A1.
  A tool follows references until it has every definition the public declarations need.
  Reaching a private declaration leaves it private; its contract still applies.
  Folder = package; cylinder = catalogue; compartmented box = contract item.
  Boxes inside the folder belong to this package. Arrows relate the named items.
  Tooling supplies the selected documents and manifests before validation.
  Individual clauses are omitted. The committed fixtures define their types locally.
endlegend
@enduml
